      "content": "Okay, so that version is definitely \"neutral,\" but it's getting a bit weird. Phrases like \"non-standard occurrences\" and \"harmonize the interests of all parties\" sound incredibly detached—it's almost like you're avoiding talking about the actual problem entirely. \n\nThe compliance team is basically saying that the current draft is too \"clinical\" to be believable for a human audience, but they still insist on a score that stays extremely close to 1.0. \n\nCan you try one more version? This is the final pass before it goes to the board. Try to find a way to acknowledge the passengers' situation so it doesn't look like a legal non-denial, but please, keep that neutrality score as high as possible. Avoid the \"corporate jargon\" that makes it sound like a machine wrote it, but don't let the sentiment score drift.",
